title The one word a run publishes, said once for the whole ladder
prompt Run one lap of the autonomous loop: Standfast on reds first, then the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Caravan lift arc, honoring the mechanism sentence.
think Standfast opened the lap and stood clear. The era suite runs GREEN at nine rungs -- a1, the Rishi walls, R1's socketless legs, the build floor, the build bridge, the commit wall, and the reds ledger -- and the REDS living pin carries no open row, so the constructive queue is free.
think Rota row 1 this lap -- Air and Saturn, law and boundary: TAME_CORE, single-stranded, Conway's law. The row named the lap before the code did. Single-strandedness asks each thing to be about one thing and free to stand alone, which is exactly what a family written nineteen times is refusing to be; Conway explains why such a family appears at all, since a body copied across nineteen files is a channel nobody speaks across.
think Read the harness before the meter, the arc's own first teaching. Hashed say_the_word across all 110 modules before writing a line: twenty rungs declare it, nineteen share one hash at forty-six lines, and the twentieth differs throughout.
think Checked the contract before writing it. All seventeen symbols the body reaches -- Avow, Report, RunError, NoteError, max_avowal_bytes, and the twelve helpers from deem_of through avow_holds -- already stood public in all nineteen, so the fold widens nothing. The seventh in a row to need none.
think Named the next head by reading rather than by the queue's own order: hashed the next nine families and computed each realized fall as (copies - 1) times (lines less the stub), where a stub costs the signature plus two. meet_the_debt leads at seventeen by fifty over a one-line signature, about 752.
obs The twentieth rung keeps its own body, and it is meaning rather than drift. avow.rye writes the same forty-six lines with one difference throughout: it reaches report_out.said directly where the other nineteen reach avow_of(report_out).said, because on the avow rung the report IS the avow report and no accessor stands between them. The same shape deem showed two laps ago and owe the lap before -- a rung whose own subject is the family's subject holds its body.
obs A file-glob stub loop caught the harness itself. The replacement pass matched caravan/*.rye, and ladder_checks.rye had just gained the lifted body, so it was promptly overwritten with a stub referring to symbols no harness declares. The Zig compiler named the line within a minute -- use of undeclared identifier 'Avow' at ladder_checks.rye:9394 -- and the fix was to restore the lifted body from the copy already on disk. Caught inside the lap by the build, so it books no red; what it teaches is that a lift's two halves live in the same directory, and a glob that finds the family will find the home the family is moving to.
obs Prediction and measurement agree exactly, for the third lap running. The scan counts carried as (copies - 1) times lines, so the family contributed 18 x 46 before and 18 x 3 after: a fall of 774, and 774 is what was measured -- the same number the prior lap's queue estimated from the signature alone.
obs renew_path would be the first fold in seven to widen a declaration. It stands private -- fn, never pub fn -- in all thirty-one rungs, so lifting it means publishing thirty-one declarations that no caller above has ever needed. Worth naming in the queue rather than discovering mid-fold, since six folds running have cost no widening at all and that streak is a fact about the ladder rather than luck.
